/// A workflow file amends this module and lists its stages:
///
///     amends "modulepath:/ruxel/Workflow.pkl"
///     import "modulepath:/ruxel/Debian.pkl" as debian
///     stages { new Stage { steps { new debian.AptPackages { names { "nginx" } } } } }
///
/// The step classes that are not tied to one system come from `Core.pkl`,
/// which this module extends, so they need no prefix. The rest live in the
/// layer modules beside it: import the ones the workflow uses and qualify
/// their classes (`linux.Timezone`, `systemd.Service`, `debian.AptPackages`).
/// pklr does not check the classes of list elements, so `internal/Lowering`
/// checks each one instead, which also refuses a class from a copied module.
open module Workflow

extends "modulepath:/ruxel/Core.pkl"

import "modulepath:/ruxel/internal/Lowering.pkl" as Lowering

/// The workflow's stable identity: a name no other workflow that runs
/// against the same machines may share. The agent namespaces its ledger
/// by it, so two workflows with the same play and host names but
/// different names never share ledger state — and two checkouts of one
/// workflow share it, because the identity travels with the workflow,
/// not the directory it was loaded from. Name it after what the
/// workflow does (for example the repository and purpose); the corpus
/// names its fixtures by their path, which is unique there for the
/// same reason. The name must not be empty: two empty-named workflows
/// with common play and target names would share one namespace, and
/// one run could consume or prune the other's ledger records. The
/// emptiness itself is refused in `internal/Lowering.pkl` as well,
/// which both evaluators enforce with the same message.
name: String(length > 0)

/// Stages run one after another.
stages: Listing<Stage>

/// Replaced by the ruxel binary that serves this module, like the engine's
/// `Config.schemaFingerprint`. Lowering checks it against its own.
hidden fixed servedFingerprint: String = "unserved"

output {
  value = Lowering.config(stages, servedFingerprint, name)
}
